WEBVTT

00:00:00.820 --> 00:00:05.820
Hi, this is Dr. Jelena Trajkovic from the CECS Department at Cal State Long Beach.

00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:12.820
So we are going to design a Moore FSM and represent it as a state transition diagram

00:00:12.820 --> 00:00:16.820
that is going to control the holiday light display.

00:00:16.820 --> 00:00:24.820
And the display is going to use three signals, S and T and U, to control the light bulbs.

00:00:24.820 --> 00:00:30.760
Each transition from state to state is going to be happening on clock and each

00:00:30.760 --> 00:00:35.480
time that we have transition on that clock the light display is going to

00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:43.800
change its state. So now we have to design that we have a sequence that is

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:54.520
desired as having 0 0 0 1 0 1 0 1 0 1 1 1 and then we start over again 0 0 0 1

00:00:54.520 --> 00:00:56.560
one, zero, one, and so on and so forth.

00:00:56.560 --> 00:00:58.980
So basically kind of we are wrapping around

00:00:58.980 --> 00:00:59.820
to some extent.

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:05.020
So we need to make a FSM.

00:01:05.020 --> 00:01:08.800
Each state is going to be assigned

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:12.700
to one desired combination of these light switches.

00:01:12.700 --> 00:01:15.520
So first of all, how many states do we have?

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:17.900
If you look into,

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:20.900
let me change the color.

00:01:20.900 --> 00:01:28.520
If you look into the desired display order,

00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:36.300
we see that we have four different possibilities for output,

00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:40.380
and then we repeat state zero,

00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:43.020
and then state one, state two, state three.

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:46.060
The other thing is how are ST and U mapped?

00:01:46.060 --> 00:01:50.220
Well, we are going to say that the first leftmost

00:01:50.220 --> 00:01:53.780
the most significant bit is going to represent S,

00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:57.540
the second one T, the third one U.

00:01:57.540 --> 00:02:02.540
So it's STU, STU, and STU.

00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:12.850
Now let's go and design our state transition diagram.

00:02:12.850 --> 00:02:14.730
So we have four possibilities.

00:02:14.730 --> 00:02:16.970
We're going to have four states.

00:02:16.970 --> 00:02:21.370
And we can easily call them S0, S1, S2, and S3.

00:02:21.370 --> 00:02:31.640
S0. Again, starting state is not defined for us in the problem, so we can

00:02:31.640 --> 00:02:39.360
make a decision where what is going to be our starting state. And we are

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:45.720
going to go from S0, and S0 is going to have outputs 0, 0, 0. So this is

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:57.520
respectively S, T and U value. From that state we're going to go to state S1

00:02:57.520 --> 00:03:09.240
that has 1 0 1. What are we going to consult for transition? Actually

00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:18.120
nothing. So this is one kind of corner case where on the clock edge we will be

00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:22.920
transitioning. So we are not going to ask if there is any variable, if the variable

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:29.920
has particular value. We are going to say on the next clock transition, sorry, on

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:34.720
next positive edge of the clock, we are going to transition in the next state

00:03:34.720 --> 00:03:41.080
and state is S1. On the next positive edges of clock we are going to

00:03:41.080 --> 00:03:51.400
transition to S2 that is going to have 0 1 0 and on the following one we can go

00:03:51.400 --> 00:04:00.680
to S3 that has 1 1 1. Once we are done with S3 we're going to go and wrap

00:04:00.680 --> 00:04:08.920
around and start S1 again. We'll do a little bit more complex problems in

00:04:08.920 --> 00:04:16.620
class and move to state encoding next time. Thanks for watching!

